<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivor Wilks</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1928">1928</a>–<a href="/years/2014">2014</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ivor G. Wilks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivor Wilks was a British Africanist and historian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, considered one of the founders of modern African historiography. After service in the British Army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> and studies at Bangor and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, he went in 1953 to the University College of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>, now the University of Ghana, where he devoted his career to what he called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a> of West African history and helped set up the Institute of African Studies at Legon. In 1966 he moved to Northwestern University. His 1975 book on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> in the nineteenth century remains a standard text on the Ashanti Empire; he also wrote on the Welsh working-class movement.</p></div>
